WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.780
Your phone shows three ways

2
00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.050
to get across town.

3
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.110
One has the fewest turns.

4
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.530
Another is shortest in kilometres.

5
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.790
The third is fastest

6
00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:14.700
with the traffic as it is now.

7
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.100
Each road has a number on it

8
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.475
the time to drive it.

9
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.803
The fastest route is the one where those

10
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:22.850
numbers add up to the least.

11
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.450
Last time

12
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.630
breadth-first search counted edges

13
00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.650
and gave us the route with the fewest.

14
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.230
It ignored the numbers.

15
00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.200
Today we use them.

16
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.820
This is Dijkstra's algorithm.

17
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.780
We'll see the idea of tentative distances

18
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.240
a step-by-step run on our example

19
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.650
and why it works, and when it breaks.

20
00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.919
Start from A, with distance zero.

21
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.450
Every other vertex gets a tentative

22
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.830
distance of infinity: we know no route yet.

23
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.730
Repeat one step.

24
00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.948
Among the vertices not yet settled

25
00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.675
pick the one with the smallest

26
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.210
tentative distance.

27
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.980
Settle it: its distance is final.

28
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.917
Then look at each of its edges.

29
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.408
If going through this vertex gives a neighbour

30
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.740
a cheaper route than it has now

31
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.140
update the neighbour.

32
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.530
That update is called relaxing the edge.

33
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.281
To pick the closest vertex quickly

34
00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.876
keep the tentative distances

35
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.280
in a priority queue, a heap.

36
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.950
Relaxing an edge is one comparison.

37
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.830
We have settled u, with distance d.

38
00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.020
The edge from u to v has weight w.

39
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.476
If d plus w is less than the distance

40
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.160
we have recorded for v

41
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.360
we have found a better route.

42
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.770
Record it, and remember u as v's parent.

43
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.469
Watch for the word tentative.

44
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.739
A vertex can be relaxed several

45
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.739
times before it is settled.

46
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.133
Its first distance is only the

47
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.080
best route found so far.

48
00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.850
Our example.

49
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.950
Settle A, at zero.

50
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.180
Relax its edges: B gets six, D gets three.

51
00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.540
The closest unsettled vertex is D, at three.

52
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:08.520
Settle it.

53
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.130
Relax: E gets sixteen, F gets eight.

54
00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.650
Next is B, at six.

55
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.110
Relax: C gets fourteen.

56
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.990
And E: six plus seven is thirteen

57
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.570
which beats sixteen.

58
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.790
E improves, to thirteen

59
00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.880
with B as its new parent.

60
00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.060
Next is F, at eight.

61
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.350
G gets twenty.

62
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.350
Then E, at thirteen, and C, at fourteen.

63
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.670
They have nothing left to improve.

64
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.020
Finally G, at twenty.

65
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:47.180
The final distances: A zero, D three, B six

66
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:53.490
F eight, E thirteen, C fourteen, G twenty.

67
00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.723
There is a catch.

68
00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.155
A heap cannot change the priority of

69
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.790
an entry that is already inside it.

70
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.770
So when E improved from sixteen to thirteen

71
00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.070
we added a second entry for E

72
00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.500
and left the old one in the heap.

73
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.200
Later the old entry, E at sixteen

74
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.140
comes to the front.

75
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.780
E is already settled

76
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.240
so we recognise it as stale and skip it.

77
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.010
This trick is called lazy deletion.

78
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.470
In our run, eight pushes, eight pops

79
00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.880
one of them stale.

80
00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.530
Every time we improved a vertex

81
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.970
we remembered its parent.

82
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.690
To get the route to G

83
00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:36.140
follow the parents back: F, then D, then A.

84
00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:41.180
The route is A, D, F, G, cost twenty.

85
00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.720
Breadth-first search gave us A, B, E, G

86
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.160
cost twenty-three.

87
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.690
Same number of edges, different cost.

88
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.430
The parents of all the vertices form a tree

89
00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.150
the shortest-path tree.

90
00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.040
In our example its weight is forty-one.

91
00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.288
Why is the settled distance

92
00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.150
really the shortest?

93
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.830
Suppose we settle u

94
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:06.340
the closest unsettled vertex, at distance d.

95
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.928
Any other route to u must leave

96
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.440
the settled region somewhere

97
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.820
through a vertex that is not settled yet.

98
00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.720
That vertex is at least as far as u

99
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.570
because u was the closest.

100
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.496
And the rest of the route can

101
00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.090
only add to the total

102
00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.490
because weights are not negative.

103
00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.000
So no other route can beat d.

104
00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.125
The argument uses one assumption

105
00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.870
in an essential way: no negative weights.

106
00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.204
Here is what goes wrong

107
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.800
with a negative edge.

108
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.940
From S, an edge to A of weight two

109
00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.960
an edge to B of weight three

110
00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.340
an edge from A to C of weight three

111
00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.530
and an edge from B back to A of minus two.

112
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.070
Dijkstra settles A at two

113
00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.350
and relaxes C to five.

114
00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:53.921
Then it settles B, at three

115
00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.842
and finds that A would cost

116
00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:57.790
only one through B.

117
00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:00.030
Too late: A is already settled

118
00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.840
and C has already been priced through A.

119
00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.600
It reports C at five.

120
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.920
But the route S, B, A, C costs three

121
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.839
minus two, plus three: four.

122
00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.534
The settled vertex A was

123
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:15.790
never looked at again.

124
00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.250
A different algorithm, Bellman–Ford

125
00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.430
handles negative edges, at a higher cost.

126
00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.330
For non-negative weights

127
00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:24.540
Dijkstra is the tool.

128
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.010
What does it cost?

129
00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.930
Each edge is relaxed at most once

130
00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.548
when its tail is settled.

131
00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.393
Each relaxation can add

132
00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.360
one entry to the heap.

133
00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.646
A heap operation costs the

134
00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.380
logarithm of its size.

135
00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.680
So the total is on the order of V plus E

136
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.550
times log V.

137
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:45.912
Real numbers

138
00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.302
for a graph of a thousand vertices

139
00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.620
and three thousand edges

140
00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.073
one thousand six hundred

141
00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:53.390
three pushes and pops

142
00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.230
of which six hundred three were stale.

143
00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.430
A few thousand heap operations

144
00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:58.620
not millions.

145
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.370
To sum up.

146
00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.510
Keep a tentative distance for every vertex.

147
00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.250
Repeatedly settle the closest one

148
00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.840
and relax its edges.

149
00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.220
A heap makes the choice fast

150
00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.960
lazy deletion handles improved entries.

151
00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.400
Parents give the route.

152
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.550
All of this needs non-negative weights.

153
00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.349
Next: connecting everything as

154
00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:20.810
cheaply as possible

155
00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.820
with minimum spanning trees.

156
00:06:23.447 --> 00:06:26.167
Let's write Dijkstra's algorithm in Java.

157
00:06:26.747 --> 00:06:28.026
First a check

158
00:06:28.117 --> 00:06:30.000
negative weights throw an Illegal

159
00:06:30.057 --> 00:06:31.140
Argument Exception

160
00:06:31.197 --> 00:06:32.643
because the algorithm would

161
00:06:32.697 --> 00:06:34.067
give wrong answers.

162
00:06:34.417 --> 00:06:37.567
Every distance starts at Integer max value

163
00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:40.847
our infinity, except the source, at zero.

164
00:06:40.847 --> 00:06:43.467
The heap is a Priority Queue of entries

165
00:06:43.467 --> 00:06:46.367
a small record with a distance and a vertex

166
00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:49.627
and a compare to that orders by distance

167
00:06:49.627 --> 00:06:51.097
then by vertex.

168
00:06:51.447 --> 00:06:52.899
We only ever add a weight

169
00:06:52.957 --> 00:06:54.477
to a finite distance

170
00:06:54.477 --> 00:06:57.927
so the infinity value cannot overflow.

171
00:06:58.507 --> 00:07:00.957
Poll gives the closest entry.

172
00:07:00.957 --> 00:07:03.637
If the vertex is already done

173
00:07:03.637 --> 00:07:04.977
the entry is stale

174
00:07:04.977 --> 00:07:07.437
so we count it and skip it.

175
00:07:07.437 --> 00:07:09.547
Otherwise we mark it done.

176
00:07:09.897 --> 00:07:11.387
For each neighbour

177
00:07:11.387 --> 00:07:14.207
if the new distance beats the recorded one

178
00:07:14.207 --> 00:07:17.047
we update the distance, record the parent

179
00:07:17.047 --> 00:07:18.697
and add a new entry.

180
00:07:19.047 --> 00:07:22.495
Priority Queue has no decrease-key either.

181
00:07:22.577 --> 00:07:24.096
Removing a specific entry

182
00:07:24.157 --> 00:07:26.037
would cost linear time

183
00:07:26.037 --> 00:07:28.687
so lazy deletion is the standard answer.

184
00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:32.026
The method path to follows parents back

185
00:07:32.097 --> 00:07:34.037
from the target and reverses the list.

186
00:07:34.037 --> 00:07:39.267
For G: A, D, F, G, cost twenty.

187
00:07:39.847 --> 00:07:41.937
Here is the real trace.

188
00:07:41.937 --> 00:07:44.257
Pop A, relax B and D.

189
00:07:44.257 --> 00:07:48.157
Pop D, relax E to sixteen and F to eight.

190
00:07:48.157 --> 00:07:53.237
Pop B, relax C, and E improves to thirteen.

191
00:07:53.237 --> 00:07:53.757
Later

192
00:07:53.757 --> 00:07:56.887
the stale entry for E at sixteen is skipped.

193
00:07:57.237 --> 00:07:59.877
Eight pushes, eight pops, one stale.

194
00:08:00.457 --> 00:08:02.087
With the check turned off

195
00:08:02.087 --> 00:08:05.587
a negative edge gives C equal to five.

196
00:08:05.587 --> 00:08:08.267
Bellman–Ford, in the same class, gives four.

197
00:08:08.267 --> 00:08:10.537
The check is there for a reason.

198
00:08:11.117 --> 00:08:12.687
In the standard library

199
00:08:12.687 --> 00:08:14.527
Priority Queue is the heap.

200
00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:16.247
Give it a Comparable element

201
00:08:16.247 --> 00:08:17.767
or a Comparator.

202
00:08:17.767 --> 00:08:20.307
Its iterator is not sorted

203
00:08:20.307 --> 00:08:22.517
only poll returns the minimum.

204
00:08:23.097 --> 00:08:24.587
So in Java

205
00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:27.627
Priority Queue with lazy deletion

206
00:08:27.627 --> 00:08:29.307
parents for the route

207
00:08:29.307 --> 00:08:31.337
and no negative weights.

208
00:08:31.687 --> 00:08:34.607
Next: minimum spanning trees.

